   "id": "pmt/pure-core-1/ch3/q5",
   "question": "<ol class=\"parts\" type=\"a\"><li>Using the formula for <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\sum_{r=1}^{n}r^2\"></span> write down, in terms of <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"n\"></span> only, an expression for<div class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\\sum_{r=1}^{3n}r^2\" data-display=\"true\"></div><span class=\"marks\">(1)</span></li><li>Show that, for all integers <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"n\"></span>, where <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"n&gt;0\"></span><div class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\\sum_{r=2n+1}^{3n}r^2=\\frac{n}{6}(an^2+bn+c)\" data-display=\"true\"></div><p>where the values of the constants <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"a\"></span>, <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"b\"></span> and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"c\"></span> are to be found.</p><span class=\"marks\">(4)</span></li></ol>",
